 <details class="secondary-content"><summary>What am I seeing?</summary><p>This is an approximate calculation from our fixed graphene Hamiltonian. The surface shows where a declared wave packet is more likely to be detected. Its height is a plotted probability density, not the height of the graphene sheet or a particle path.</p><p>The window shows part of a larger calculation. “In view” is the probability inside this window; the total probability across the full calculation stays at one. Some probability moves beyond the visible area.</p><p>A femtosecond (fs) is one million-billionth of a second. At the default 4× viewing speed, each 200 fs interval takes 7.5 seconds. Six plays plus brief fades give a 49-second presentation, then stop. All 601 density frames are calculated from saved eigenstates. During each fade, the physical clock reads “Replay” while it resets; the fade adds no simulated evolution.</p><p id="graphene-scale"></p><p id="graphene-extents"></p><p id="graphene-frame"></p></details>
